<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ptolemaeus of Commagene</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 130 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontid-dynasty">Orontid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ptolemaeus was the founder of the kingdom of Commagene in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. A Seleucid governor of the region, he broke away from the weakening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> around 163 BCE and ruled as an independent king until about 130 BCE. His descendants, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-i-callinicus">Mithridates I Callinicus</a> and Antiochus I Theos, ruled Commagene until it came under Roman control.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ptolemaeus belonged to, or claimed descent from, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontid-dynasty">Orontid dynasty</a>, an Iranian family that had long dominated the region and traced its line to the Achaemenid satraps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. His father is unknown; the Nemrud Dağ inscriptions name his paternal grandfather as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsames-i">Arsames I</a>, king of Sophene, and inscribed reliefs found there make him a descendant of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. He was also a relative of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-i-of-parthia">Mithridates I of Parthia</a>.</p><p>Antiochus III had separated Commagene from the Kingdom of Sophene before the Roman–Seleucid war of 192–188 BCE. After the Peace of Apamea in 188 BCE stripped the Seleucid state of its lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a>, Commagene, lying east of them, stayed within the shrunken Seleucid sphere, probably as an eparchy, as the ending -ene of its name suggests.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> calls Ptolemaeus an epistates, a royal official who oversaw a city's administration, keeping the peace, carrying out royal decrees and collecting taxes. Some historians think the word may be an error for eparchos, which would make him governor of all Commagene rather than overseer of its principal city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samosata">Samosata</a>, the only significant city of Commagene, served as its capital. Coins imitating those of the Seleucid king Demetrius were struck there, and without Seleucid bullion they soon lost weight and purity in a remote and barely Hellenized province.</p><p>Ptolemaeus moved to enlarge his territory by invading Melitene, a fertile district of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> then part of the Cappadocian Kingdom. He occupied several strategic positions but was forced back into his own province when Ariarathes marched against him with a superior force.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ptolemaeus died in 130 BCE; the name of his wife is unknown. His son Sames II Theosebes Dikaios succeeded him, and his descendant Antiochus I of Commagene listed him among his paternal ancestors in the inscribed reliefs of Nemrud Dağ, where he is regarded as the founder of the kingdom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ptolemaeus of Commagene” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its governor <b>Ptolemaeus</b> broke away from the Seleucid Empire c. 163 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-commagene">Kingdom of Commagene</a></li></ul></section>
